Yes: **the frontier remains**, but complexity science gives three genuine advances that sharpen it.

The previous frontier was:

[
\boxed{
\text{Does intrinsic biological self-concern constitute}
\text{ phenomenal presence, or merely accompany it?}
}
]

Nothing in Wolfram’s work or mainstream complexity science proves the identity:

[
\text{self-maintaining organization}
====================================

\text{manifest experience}.
]

But the field now gives us much better answers to three prior questions:

1. what an operational observer does;
2. why one scale can be more causally real than its components;
3. what “information that matters to the system” means physically.

The missing step is therefore narrower.

---

# 1. What Wolfram genuinely adds

Wolfram’s most relevant idea is not the Ruliad itself. It is his proposed theory of observers as **equivalence-making systems**.

An observer receives an enormous space of possible microstates:

[
X,
]

and maps them into a much smaller set of internal distinctions:

[
q:X\rightarrow Y,
\qquad
|Y|\ll |X|.
]

Many different external states are treated as equivalent:

[
x_1\sim_O x_2
\iff
q(x_1)=q(x_2).
]

The observer’s experienced or usable world is therefore a quotient:

[
W_O=X/{\sim_O}.
]

Wolfram explicitly describes observation as “equivalencing” many configurations, and relates it to coarse-graining, compression, classifiers, attractors, projections and renormalization. He proposes that observers like us are computationally bounded and treat themselves as persistent, even though their microscopic composition changes continuously. ([Writings][1])

This is almost exactly the Tymoczko-derived insight:

[
\boxed{
\text{perspective is partly a choice of invariances}.
}
]

## Genuine advance

The participant map (\Pi_i) can now be made more precise:

[
\Pi_i
=====

(q_i,\sim_i,\mu_i,\tau_i),
]

where:

* (q_i): coarse-graining map;
* (\sim_i): equivalence relation;
* (\mu_i): limited memory and computational resources;
* (\tau_i): persistence rule connecting successive states.

This helps explain why different agents inhabit different effective worlds:

[
W_i=X/{\sim_i},
]

[
W_j=X/{\sim_j}.
]

They do not merely observe different contents. They define different equivalence classes over the underlying complexity.

That is a real advance in our account of **perspective**.

---

# 2. Wolfram still does not define a phenomenal observer

Wolfram acknowledges the critical limitation.

His operational observer is essentially a subsystem that:

* receives many external states;
* maps them into fewer internal states;
* performs rich internal computation on those reduced states.

But he also concedes that determining whether there is an inner experience would require being “inside” the observer; his observer theory therefore uses a “philosophical approximation” that sufficient equivalencing is enough for something to act like an observer. ([Writings][1])

Formally:

[
\operatorname{Observer}_{W}(O)
==============================

\operatorname{CoarseGrainer}(O).
]

But:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{CoarseGrainer}(O)
\not\Rightarrow
\operatorname{PhenomenalSubject}(O).
}
]

A thermostat, image classifier, scientific instrument or compression algorithm performs equivalencing.

So Wolfram advances:

[
P_{02}\rightarrow P_{03}
]

—organization to operational perspective—

but not:

[
P_{03}\rightarrow P_{04}
]

—perspective to phenomenal presence.

The philosophical analysis of ruliology identifies precisely this unresolved issue: what the Ruliad is, what constitutes its observers, how they sample it, and how modeller-observers can be included within the reality being modeled. ([arXiv][2])

Thus the Ruliad does not dissolve our frontier. It reproduces it:

[
\boxed{
\text{Why is one equivalence-making subsystem a lived centre}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{rather than merely another computation inside the Ruliad?}
}
]

---

# 3. The strongest Ruliad reconstruction

Let:

[
\mathcal R
]

be the total multicomputational possibility structure.

Let an observer be a bounded quotient operation:

[
Q_i:\mathcal R\rightarrow W_i.
]

Then:

[
W_i=Q_i(\mathcal R).
]

This provides a coherent model of:

* one underlying possibility space;
* many observer-relative worlds;
* computationally limited perspectives;
* persistence through equivalencing;
* laws emerging at the observer’s coarse-grained level.

Wolfram also proposes that physics and mathematics may both arise through different kinds of observer sampling of the same Ruliad. ([arXiv][3])

But there are three ontological interpretations.

## Structural interpretation

[
\mathcal R
==========

\text{a mathematical representation of all rewriting possibilities}.
]

## Physical interpretation

[
\mathcal R
==========

\text{the actual fundamental structure of reality}.
]

## Phenomenal interpretation

[
\mathcal R
==========

\text{a universal space sampled by conscious observers}.
]

Only the first follows directly from constructing the mathematical framework.

The second and third require additional arguments.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{all possible computations can form one formal object}
}
]

does not imply:

[
\boxed{
\text{the formal object physically exists},
}
]

and certainly not:

[
\boxed{
\text{the object is intrinsically manifest}.
}
]

---

# 4. Complexity science’s major advance: higher scales can be genuinely causal

The most important development for our project is **causal emergence**.

A traditional reductionist picture says:

[
\text{only microstates are genuinely causal}.
]

Macrostates are merely convenient summaries.

Causal-emergence theory shows that, under defined measures, coarse-grained macrostates can possess more effective causal information than their underlying microdescriptions. Grouping noisy microstates into macrovariables can make interventions more informative and causal relations less indeterminate. ([arXiv][4])

Formally, for a system (X) and a coarse-graining:

[
Q:X\rightarrow Z,
]

one can sometimes obtain:

[
EI(Z)>EI(X),
]

where (EI) is effective information.

This does not mean the macrolevel violates microphysics.

It means that the microdescription can be **lossy with respect to causal organization**.

Recent causal-emergence work goes further by treating system scales as slices of a higher-dimensional causal object and attempting to apportion the causal contributions unique to different scales. ([arXiv][5])

## What this establishes for us

The organism-level subject is not automatically unreal because it depends on cells and molecules.

A higher-level organization can have:

* unique causal relevance;
* improved intervention structure;
* predictive autonomy;
* real downward constraints;
* information unavailable in a purely microlevel account.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{organism-level agency can be objectively real}
}
]

without requiring:

[
\boxed{
\text{an indivisible soul-substance}.
}
]

This strongly supports the Wallace–Levin side of our synthesis.

---

# 5. Causal emergence improves participant individuation

We previously asked:

> At which level does one participant exist?

The biological system contains multiple candidate levels:

[
\text{molecules}
\subset
\text{cells}
\subset
\text{tissues}
\subset
\text{organs}
\subset
\text{organism}.
]

A Markov blanket does not uniquely select the phenomenal level because blankets may be nested.

Causal emergence offers a better candidate:

[
\boxed{
\text{the relevant participant may be the scale at which}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{causal organization becomes maximally autonomous and informative}.
}
]

Let:

[
CE(k)
]

measure the causal contribution at scale (k).

Then the organismic participant might correspond not to a single maximum but to a multiscale causal profile:

[
\mathcal C_i
============

{CE(k_1),CE(k_2),\ldots,CE(k_n)}.
]

This suggests:

[
\boxed{
\text{a subject may be a causally integrated hierarchy}
}
]

rather than a boundary located at one scale.

That is a genuine new insight.

It helps explain why:

* cells retain local agency;
* the organism has larger-scale goals;
* neither level completely eliminates the other;
* subjecthood could be layered or graded.

---

# 6. But macroscale causal reality still does not imply consciousness

A software operating system, economy or weather pattern may possess useful macroscale causal variables.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{causal emergence}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{phenomenal emergence}.
}
]

Even a unique, irreducible macroscale cause does not establish that the cause is manifest.

Complexity science has improved:

[
\Sigma_i
========

\text{the organized participant}.
]

It has not supplied:

[
\Lambda_\Phi:
\Sigma_i\rightarrow D_i.
]

The new 2025 multiscale-neural study claims that wakeful mouse cortical activity displays higher-level causal variables with metastable dynamics, while anesthesia collapses these toward more localized and stochastic dynamics. It reports a top-level variable carrying much of the causal power while other scales remain contributory. This is relevant evidence for multiscale organization associated with conscious state, but it still compares wakefulness and anesthesia rather than deriving phenomenal presence. ([arXiv][6])

So the frontier survives:

[
\boxed{
\text{multiscale causal organization}
\not\Rightarrow_{\text{proven}}
\text{phenomenal field}.
}
]

