# 7. Semantic information: the sharpest advance toward “mattering”

This may be the most important complexity-science result for our exact frontier.

Shannon information measures statistical correlation:

[
I(X;Y).
]

But not every correlation matters to the system.

Kolchinsky and Wolpert define **semantic information** as the portion of correlations between a system and its environment that is causally necessary for the system to maintain its own existence. The definition uses counterfactual interventions that scramble correlations and measures the resulting effect on the system’s viability. ([arXiv][7])

Let:

* (S): system;
* (E): environment;
* (V(S)): viability measure;
* (I(S;E)): system–environment mutual information.

Scramble some correlation:

[
I(S;E)\rightarrow I'(S;E).
]

If this reduces viability:

[
V'(S)<V(S),
]

then the removed correlation carried semantic information for the system.

This gives us a rigorous definition of:

[
\boxed{
\text{information that matters to the system itself}.
}
]

Not “meaning” imposed by a human interpreter.

Not arbitrary Shannon information.

Rather:

[
\boxed{
\text{a correlation has meaning insofar as the system}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{needs it to remain within its viable state-space}.
}
]

Resource-gathering models have demonstrated semantic thresholds: below a certain level of sensor noise, the agent retains enough information to survive; beyond the threshold, expected lifetime drops sharply. ([arXiv][8])

