# 9. The strongest new frontier is valence, not generic integration

Previously we asked:

[
\text{Why is integrated organization manifest?}
]

That may be too broad.

Complexity science suggests a more discriminating question:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{When does causal information become normatively indexed}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{to the continued existence of a particular system—and}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{when does that normative structure become felt?}
}
]

Integration is common.

Self-modeling is common.

Coarse-graining is common.

But **intrinsic stakes** may be far more specific.

A living system has states that are objectively:

[
\text{viable}
\quad\text{or}\quad
\text{nonviable}
]

relative to its own continuation.

This creates an endogenous norm:

[
V_i(x).
]

Prediction errors and environmental signals are then not merely differences. They have organism-relative consequences:

[
\Delta V_i\neq0.
]

That gives an objective foundation for:

* attraction;
* avoidance;
* salience;
* concern;
* value;
* action.

The great unknown is whether:

[
\boxed{
\text{endogenous normativity}
=============================

\text{primitive phenomenal valence}.
}
]

This is now more specific than the generic hard problem.

