# 10. Computational mechanics contributes predictive individuality

Computational mechanics defines causal states by grouping past histories that yield the same probability distribution over futures:

[
x_{-\infty:t}
\sim_\epsilon
x'_{-\infty:t}
]

when:

[
P(X_{t:\infty}\mid x_{-\infty:t})
=================================

P(X_{t:\infty}\mid x'_{-\infty:t}).
]

The resulting (\epsilon)-machine is the minimal predictive model of the process.

The important insight is that a system’s effective state is not necessarily its microscopic present.

It is an equivalence class of histories with the same future consequences.

That yields:

[
\boxed{
\text{identity can be predictive equivalence across histories}.
}
]

Recent work applies (\epsilon)-machines to agent-based models to identify recurrent behavioral regimes and intrinsic computation generated by agent interactions. ([arXiv][9])

This strengthens our account of persistence:

[
\Gamma_i
========

\text{causal-state trajectory},
]

rather than:

[
\Gamma_i
========

\text{unchanging substance}.
]

A subject’s continuity may therefore consist in the preservation of a predictive organization:

[
\epsilon_i(t)
\rightarrow
\epsilon_i(t+\Delta t).
]

But again:

[
\boxed{
\text{minimal predictive state}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{phenomenal subject}.
}
]

