# 11. Computational irreducibility and agency

A growing speculative line argues that computational irreducibility may contribute to genuine agency: if a system’s future behavior cannot be shortcut and must be generated through the system’s own unfolding, then the system may possess a kind of causal sourcehood. A 2025 proposal explicitly develops computational irreducibility as a foundation of agency, linking it with causal emergence and complexity measures. ([arXiv][10])

The useful core is:

[
\boxed{
\text{the agent’s action may not be replaceable by a}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{substantially simpler external prediction}.
}
]

But this does not establish free will.

A cellular automaton may be computationally irreducible.

Therefore:

[
\text{irreducibility}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{agency},
]

and:

[
\text{agency}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{phenomenal authorship}.
]

At most, irreducibility may be one condition for **nontrivial autonomy**:

[
\text{the system’s trajectory must actually unfold}
]

rather than be replaceable by a smaller fixed lookup.

