# 12. Complexity science’s actual frontier

The complexity frontier is not:

> How can simple rules create complicated shapes?

That is established.

The difficult frontier is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{How do we identify objective higher-level entities,}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{their boundaries, their causal powers, their values}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{and their persistence across changing microstates?}
}
]

The leading formal tools address different parts:

| Problem                                 | Complexity tool                 |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Which scale is causally real?           | Causal emergence                |
| What information matters to the system? | Semantic information            |
| What is the minimal predictive state?   | Computational mechanics         |
| How does an observer reduce complexity? | Coarse-graining/observer theory |
| How does a system remain organized?     | Nonequilibrium thermodynamics   |
| How do levels constrain one another?    | Multiscale dynamics             |
| Why is prediction sometimes impossible? | Computational irreducibility    |
| How do stable wholes arise?             | Attractors and metastability    |

These tools provide a mathematical science of:

[
\boxed{
\text{agent formation}.
}
]

They do not yet provide a science of:

[
\boxed{
\text{manifestness}.
}
]

