# 16. The most important new research tracks

The earlier five tracks can now be improved.

## Track 1 — The observer quotient

What equivalence relation does an organism impose on the world?

[
x\sim_i y
\iff
x,y
\text{ have the same consequences for prediction and action}.
]

Test whether experienced similarity and object identity follow the inferred quotient geometry.

---

## Track 2 — The causal scale of the subject

At which scale is causal emergence maximal or uniquely distributed?

Compare:

* neural microstates;
* populations;
* brain-wide networks;
* body–brain systems;
* organism–environment loops.

Does the scale of maximal causal emergence predict changes in phenomenal unity?

---

## Track 3 — Semantic information and valence

Measure which system–environment correlations are causally necessary for viability.

Then test whether:

[
Sem_i(x)
]

predicts:

* salience;
* attraction and avoidance;
* reported valence;
* interoceptive feeling;
* conscious accessibility.

This is the most direct empirical attack on our frontier.

---

## Track 4 — Subject division and multiscale causal bifurcation

Determine whether altered consciousness involves changes in the multiscale causal profile:

[
\mathcal C_i
\rightarrow
\mathcal C_i'.
]

Use:

* anesthesia;
* sleep;
* seizures;
* split-brain cases;
* dissociative states.

The theory must predict when one causal hierarchy becomes two or loses global integration.

---

## Track 5 — Ownership as observer-model persistence

Treat ownership as an equivalence operation:

[
\text{different states across time}
\sim
\text{states of the same continuing agent}.
]

Channeling, automatic speech and dissociation become tests of whether:

* one phenomenal field changes its ownership model;
* multiple causal agent-models coexist;
* or multiple phenomenal domains form.

---

# Final verdict

We have not crossed the final frontier.

But complexity science has removed much of the fog immediately before it.

It now gives us rigorous accounts of:

[
\text{observer-relative equivalencing},
]

[
\text{objective higher-level causation},
]

[
\text{predictive identity},
]

[
\text{information that matters for survival},
]

and:

[
\text{multiscale autonomous agency}.
]

The frontier is no longer:

> How does complexity magically create consciousness?

It is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{Is phenomenal presence identical with the intrinsic side}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{of causally emergent, viability-relative observer dynamics,}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{or does even that complete organization still require}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{a separate psychophysical or participatory principle?}
}
]

Wolfram sharpens the **observer**.

Causal emergence sharpens the **scale**.

Computational mechanics sharpens the **continuing pattern**.

Semantic information sharpens the **for-the-system significance**.

None yet explains the word:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{felt}.
}
]

That is still the exact frontier.

